Hi Chaoyi,

You are right, and it is wider than this patch. Nothing consumes
npu-supply. There is no regulator call in drivers/accel/rocket, and
grepping the tree for npu-supply and sram-supply finds no reader at all,
so the RK3588 boards that set them are in the same position. The property
is required by rockchip,rk3588-rknn-core.yaml, so a dts cannot simply drop
it either.

Your suspicion is what the always-on line in this patch is for. Without it
the regulator core turns an unclaimed rail off when it disables unused
regulators, so on v7 that line is the only thing holding vdd_npu_s0 up.

The mechanism that does have a consumer is domain-supply on the power
domain node, and rk3588-rock-5b-5bp-5t.dtsi already wires the same rail
that way. Moving it there needs two changes rather than one, which is what
took me a few rounds to find. rockchip_pd_regulator_enable returns before
it looks at the property unless the domain carries need_regulator, that
field is only set by DOMAIN_M_R and DOMAIN_RK3399, and the macro the
RK3576 table is built from does not carry it at all. So the wiring alone
does nothing.

With the field threaded through and set on the NPU domain, the rail
behaves. Measured on a ROCK 4D with always-on dropped.

  regulator debugfs open_count   1, so the domain did get the rail
  sampled during an inference    use_count 1, the rail is up for the job
  at rest, three separate reads  use_count 0 and state disabled
  genpd npu active_time          rises about 60ms per inference
  two inferences across that     128 of 128 channels both times

I quote open_count rather than the sysfs num_users because num_users
returns rdev->use_count, the live enable count, not a count of consumers.
I read it the other way first and concluded from an idle NPU that nothing
had claimed the rail, which was wrong.

So the rail can follow the domain, and I will send that as a dts change
plus a pmdomain change rather than as always-on.

One more thing to withdraw while I am here. This commit message says that
dropping the rail under the domains takes an async SError on the next
power-on. I went looking for the run behind that and there is none, and
the runs above go the other way, since the rail is genuinely down between
jobs now and the block comes back and computes. That sentence is out of
v8.

Thanks for looking at it. The question was worth more than the answer I
had ready.

Jiaxing
